WEBVTT

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:01.046
One game.

00:00:01.318 --> 00:00:02.348
Three versions.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:13.648
Many games.

00:00:13.868 --> 00:00:15.083
One refinement loop.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:29.942
Recursive Game Creator refines games through experience.

00:00:30.698 --> 00:00:33.576
Designer plans experience-driven changes.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.398
Builder implements code and assets.

00:00:37.211 --> 00:00:39.967
Player writes reusable gameplay policies.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:43.116
Reviewer evaluates the playing experience.

00:00:43.762 --> 00:00:48.249
Policies collect diverse trajectories, without per-action model calls.

00:00:48.729 --> 00:00:52.308
Review combines play records, visuals, and preferences.

00:00:52.847 --> 00:00:54.992
Feedback guides the next revision.

00:00:55.865 --> 00:00:58.630
Meme Arena refines combat presentation.

00:00:59.562 --> 00:01:02.605
The first version establishes a playable arena.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.585
Later versions add a richer stage and more polished character movement.

00:01:11.662 --> 00:01:14.249
Across the same skills, added effects

00:01:14.259 --> 00:01:17.545
make each action and impact easier to read.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:26.435
The eight-bull finisher gains a stronger buildup.

00:01:33.312 --> 00:01:36.937
Then, a cinematic cut-in and a distinct final strike.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.888
Matched moves reveal how the presentation evolves.

00:01:44.412 --> 00:01:46.605
Next, Racing Rocket Trials.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:51.554
Version one warns of an explosion with a flashing barrel.

00:01:56.062 --> 00:02:00.270
Version two adds a live countdown and a marked blast range.

00:02:04.062 --> 00:02:09.411
Version three distinguishes direct crashes from knockback with separate danger zones.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.112
Recovery costs are visible before you commit.

00:02:17.111 --> 00:02:21.039
Focused section practice provides its own retry controls.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:26.746
The obstacle stays the same.

00:02:26.943 --> 00:02:28.663
The feedback becomes clearer.

00:02:30.874 --> 00:02:33.237
Whitebird evolves from an early prototype.

00:02:34.562 --> 00:02:40.226
Version one establishes movement, automatic attacks, and three starting blessings.

00:02:41.562 --> 00:02:46.656
The next build adds illustrated characters, enemies, and weapon techniques.

00:02:48.762 --> 00:02:54.074
Version three refines yokai artwork, character movement, and the combat interface.

00:02:57.562 --> 00:03:02.073
Skill details explain what each technique does, alongside the current build.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:09.318
Room routes and blessing choices give the journey more structure.

00:03:12.662 --> 00:03:16.154
The same core idea grows into a more complete game.

00:03:18.562 --> 00:03:24.056
Across three rounds, GameCraft-Bench scores improve by 5.19 points.

00:03:26.561 --> 00:03:31.854
GameASG-Bench: 53.2% strict success.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.519
Up 34.1 percentage points.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:37.901
Explore the games, and read the paper.
